{"issue":{"title":"Malformed config file error does not say which file is broken","problem":"src/config.js `loadConfig(path)` is `JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8'))`, so a typo throws a bare SyntaxError whose message never mentions the file. src/app.js catches it and prints `bad config: <message>` with exit 1. Observed on Node 22: a config at /tmp/x/config.json with `greeting: \"x\"` on line 3 gives `bad config: Expected double-quoted property name in JSON at position 20 (line 3 column 3)`. The line shows up only because this Node version puts it in its message, and the file path never appears. The README says an invalid file stops the app with `bad config: <message>` and exit 1, and test/config.test.js asserts that bad JSON throws.","reason":"When the app dies on a bad config, people can't tell which file is broken or where the mistake is, so they can't fix it quickly. This is worse when the path comes from argv or the default `config.json` is resolved against an unexpected working directory.","outcome":"A malformed config still stops the app with exit 1. The error now names the file and the line of the mistake. Callers of loadConfig still get an error for malformed JSON, and the error now names the file. Valid configs load as before.","expect":["Running `node src/app.js /tmp/cfgcheck/broken.json`, where that file contains `{\\n  \"port\": 9000,\\n  greeting: \"x\"\\n}`, exits with code 1, prints nothing to stdout, and its stderr contains the string `/tmp/cfgcheck/broken.json`.","In that same run, stderr gives the line of the mistake (line 3), either as the text `line 3` or as `/tmp/cfgcheck/broken.json:3`.","Calling `loadConfig('/tmp/cfgcheck/broken.json')` on that same file throws (it does not return `{}` or any other value), and the thrown error's `message` contains `/tmp/cfgcheck/broken.json`.","Regression: with `/tmp/cfgcheck/good.json` containing `{\"port\": 9000}`, `loadConfig` returns exactly `{ port: 9000 }`, and `node src/app.js /tmp/cfgcheck/good.json` prints `listening on 9000` and exits 0.","`npm test` exits 0, and test/ contains a test that loads a malformed config file and asserts that the error message contains that file's path."],"proofs":[{"line":"Running `node src/app.js /tmp/cfgcheck/broken.json`, where that file contains `{\\n  \"port\": 9000,\\n  greeting: \"x\"\\n}`, exits with code 1, prints nothing to stdout, and its stderr contains the string `/tmp/cfgcheck/broken.json`.","proof":"run","how":"mkdir -p /tmp/cfgcheck && printf '{\\n  \"port\": 9000,\\n  greeting: \"x\"\\n}' > /tmp/cfgcheck/broken.json && node src/app.js /tmp/cfgcheck/broken.json >/tmp/cfgcheck/out 2>/tmp/cfgcheck/err; echo exit=$?; wc -c </tmp/cfgcheck/out; grep -F /tmp/cfgcheck/broken.json /tmp/cfgcheck/err. Need: exit=1, 0 bytes of stdout, and grep matches."},{"line":"In that same run, stderr gives the line of the mistake (line 3), either as the text `line 3` or as `/tmp/cfgcheck/broken.json:3`.","proof":"run","how":"After the previous command: grep -E 'line 3([^0-9]|$)|/tmp/cfgcheck/broken\\.json:3([^0-9]|$)' /tmp/cfgcheck/err must match."},{"line":"Calling `loadConfig('/tmp/cfgcheck/broken.json')` on that same file throws (it does not return `{}` or any other value), and the thrown error's `message` contains `/tmp/cfgcheck/broken.json`.","proof":"run","how":"node --input-type=module -e \"import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; try { const r = loadConfig('/tmp/cfgcheck/broken.json'); console.log('RETURNED', JSON.stringify(r)); process.exit(2) } catch (e) { if (!String(e.message).includes('/tmp/cfgcheck/broken.json')) { console.log('NOPATH', e.message); process.exit(3) } console.log('OK') }\" must print OK and exit 0."},{"line":"Regression: with `/tmp/cfgcheck/good.json` containing `{\"port\": 9000}`, `loadConfig` returns exactly `{ port: 9000 }`, and `node src/app.js /tmp/cfgcheck/good.json` prints `listening on 9000` and exits 0.","proof":"run","how":"mkdir -p /tmp/cfgcheck && printf '{\"port\": 9000}' > /tmp/cfgcheck/good.json && node --input-type=module -e \"import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; import assert from 'node:assert/strict'; assert.deepEqual(loadConfig('/tmp/cfgcheck/good.json'), {port: 9000}); console.log('OK')\" && node src/app.js /tmp/cfgcheck/good.json; echo exit=$?. Need: OK, then `listening on 9000`, then exit=0."},{"line":"`npm test` exits 0, and test/ contains a test that loads a malformed config file and asserts that the error message contains that file's path.","proof":"run","how":"Run `npm test` and check it exits 0. Then read test/*.test.js and confirm one test writes malformed JSON to a file, calls loadConfig on it, and asserts that the error message includes that file's path."}],"non_goals":["Returning `{}` or defaults for a malformed config, which would hide the mistake.","Changing how a missing or unreadable config file is handled.","Validating config keys or values (schema checking).","Accepting relaxed JSON formats (JSON5, comments, trailing commas)."],"assumptions":["The request suggests catching the parse error in loadConfig and returning `{}`. That would silently run the app with defaults. It conflicts with the reported need (tell people which file is broken and where) and with the README's documented `bad config:` exit 1. So a malformed file must still be an error, now with the file path in it. Returning `{}` is not accepted.","The README's `bad config: <message>` wording and exit code 1 stay. Only the message content gets richer.","\"Where the mistake is\" means at least the line number. It can be shown as `line N` or `path:N`. A column is welcome but not required.","Whether loadConfig throws a SyntaxError or another Error subclass is left to the builder, as long as it throws and its message names the file. The existing test may be updated to match."]}}